# The RAND Health Insurance Experiment: randomizing the price of care
subtitle: "Why RAND randomized the price of care rather than coverage, what free care did to spending and to health, and how to read its famous null."
tags: ["cost sharing","demand for medical care","selection bias","health economics"]
teaches: ["methods/randomization","concepts/selection-bias","concepts/price-elasticity-of-demand","methods/reading-null-results"]
voices: {"a":"gemini:Achird | clear, warm economist who thinks aloud and explains with care; steady, lively pace, just a touch slower than brisk; never slow, never rushed","b":"gemini:Kore | sharp, friendly co-host: asks what a smart listener would, pushes back with real objections, sometimes explains; dry wit, never exaggerated; steady, lively pace, never slow"}
next: {"title":"Which average effect are you even estimating?","href":"https://drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/dcast/causal-inference-in-economics-evidence-from-health-and-health-care/which-average-effect-are-you-even-estimating.cast"}

## Who buys the generous plan?
voice: male

    math h_plan tex D_i size 46 x 350 y 690 hidden true
    math h_y1 tex Y_i(1) size 46 colors {"Y_i(1)":"#2f6b8f"} x 580 y 690 hidden true
