    text avg_t "mean 350" #8a5fa8 x 990 y 355 font_size 58 hidden true
    math eq tex "{3000} - {400} = 2600" size 54 colors {"{3000}":"#2f6b8f","{400}":"#b5482e","{500}":"#8a5fa8","{2100}":"#d0865f","{E[\\tau_i \\mid D_i{=}1]}":"#8a5fa8","{E[Y_i(0) \\mid D_i{=}1] - E[Y_i(0) \\mid D_i{=}0]}":"#d0865f","{E[\\tau_i]}":"#8a5fa8"} x 562 y 190 hidden true
    figure table aspect 3:2 members ["h_plan","h_y1","h_y1w","h_y0","h_y0w","h_t","rule_h","r1_name","r1_plan","r1_y1","r1_y0","r1_q0","r1_t","r1_qt","r2_name","r2_plan","r2_y1","r2_q1","r2_y0","r2_t","r2_qt","rule_a","avg_t","eq"] hidden true

    card title "Who buys the generous plan?"

People with generous health insurance use far more medical care. Is that because care is c (@highlight h_y1 h_y0@)heap for them, or because people who expect big bills buy generous plans?
    draw h_y1 h_y1w h_y0 h_y0w h_t rule_h

Take two families, with numbers made up to keep it plain: the Lenas expect a knee oper (@draw r2_name@)ation, the Toms nothing much.
    draw r1_name

With free care, the  (@draw r1_y1@)Lenas would spend three thousand dollars. Paying ninety-five percent of (@draw r1_y0@) every bill, two thousand five hundred.

The Toms would spend six hundred with free care, and four hundred paying almost everything themselves.
    draw r2_y1 r2_y0

So the price does matter: free care adds five hundred dollars for the Lena (@draw r2_t@)s and two hundred for the Toms.
    draw r1_t

On average, three hundred and fifty dollars a family (@highlight avg_t effect circle@). That is the number we are after.
    draw rule_a avg_t

But families choose their plans. Call the generous one D equ (@highlight r1_name@)als one: the Lenas, with the o (@draw r2_plan@)peration coming, choose it, and the Toms take the cheap one.
    draw h_plan r1_plan

So we only ever see the L (@draw r1_q0 r2_q1 parallel true@)enas with free care, and the Tom (@hide r1_t r2_t@)s paying  (@draw r1_qt r2_qt parallel true@)their own bills.
    hide r1_y0 r2_y1

    quiz "Compare what we can see: the Lenas' spending minus the Toms'. How will that gap compare with the true three hundred and fifty?"
        intro: Before the sum, a prediction.
        right: Far above: the Lenas would spend a lot on any plan, so their bill carries the knee operation as well as the price.
        wrong: Which family chose the generous plan: the one with a big bill coming, or the other?
        * About three hundred and fifty
        + Far above three hundred and fifty
        * Below three hundred and fifty

Three thousand against four hund (@highlight r1_y1 r2_y0 effect circle@)red: a gap of two thousand six hundred dollars.
    draw eq

Only five hundred of it is the price, the effect on the Lenas. The other two thousan (@hide r1_q0@) (@show r1_y0@)d one hu (@highlight r1_y0 r2_y0 effect circle@)ndred is the operation, which they would pay for on any plan.
    morph eq tex "2600 = {500} + {2100}"

That second piece is selection: it measures who buys generous cover, not what the co (@hide r1_y0@) (@show r1_q0@)ver does.
    highlight eq part "{2100}" effect underline

In general, the gap between plans is the effect on th (@highlight eq part "{E[\\tau_i \\mid D_i{=}1]}" effect underline@)ose who chose the generous one, plus how differently the two groups would spend on the same plan.
    morph eq tex "E[Y_i \\mid D_i{=}1] - E[Y_i \\mid D_i{=}0] = {E[\\tau_i \\mid D_i{=}1]} + {E[Y_i(0) \\mid D_i{=}1] - E[Y_i(0) \\mid D_i{=}0]}"

No survey of insurance plans can pull the two apart, because a family's choice of plan already carries its own forecast of its bills.
    highlight eq part "{E[Y_i(0) \\mid D_i{=}1] - E[Y_i(0) \\mid D_i{=}0]}" effect underline

Unless someone else chooses the plan, by lottery. Then the two groups would spend alike on the same plan, so the selection term falls to zero.
    highlight r1_plan r2_plan effect circle

And the gap between the plans becomes the average effect, th (@highlight avg_t effect circle@)e three hundred and fifty we were after.
    morph eq tex "E[Y_i \\mid D_i{=}1] - E[Y_i \\mid D_i{=}0] = {E[\\tau_i]}"

In the nineteen seventies, the RAND Corporation did exactly that.
    highlight h_plan

## Four prices, one lottery
voice: male
use: equation_plot
with:
    equation: y = 837 + 182*exp(-x/12) - 1.36*x
    params:
      p: {value: 0, min: 0, max: 95, label: share of the bill paid}
    x_range:
      - 0
      - 100
    y_range:
      - 600
      - 1100
    show_equation: false
    x_label: you pay (%)
    y_label: spending per person ($)
    drag: false
    marks:
      - {kind: point, at: p, curve: 0, label: false, id: you}
sources:
    - id: newhouse1993
      title: Free for All? Lessons from the RAND Health Insurance Experiment
      authors: Newhouse JP and the Insurance Experiment Group (Harvard University Press)
      year: 1993
      finding: 'Annual medical spending per person (1991 dollars, excluding dental and outpatient psychotherapy), as reported in CRS report RL33296: free care $1,019; 25% coinsurance $826; 50% $764; 95% $700.'
    - id: manning1987
      title: 'Health Insurance and the Demand for Medical Care: Evidence from a Randomized Experiment'
      authors: Manning WG, Newhouse JP, Duan N, Keeler EB, Leibowitz A, Marquis MS (American Economic Review)
      year: 1987
      finding: Spending per person on the free plan was about 45 percent higher than on the 95 percent coinsurance plan (out-of-pocket capped at $1,000 a year); price elasticity of demand about -0.2.
    - id: arondine2013
      title: The RAND Health Insurance Experiment, Three Decades Later
      authors: Aron-Dine A, Einav L, Finkelstein A (Journal of Economic Perspectives)
      year: 2013
      finding: 'Of families offered a plan, 88 percent completed the experiment on free care against 63 percent on the 95 percent plan, mostly because more refused to enrol. Free care vs 95%: share with any spending 93% vs 76%, share with any inpatient use 10.3% vs 7.9%; total spending gap many standard errors from zero. The 50% plan was small and its spending estimate imprecise (in their table above the 25% plan). After worst-case bounds, any-use and log-spending responses remain; the level of spending and inpatient use no longer differ significantly. Simple pairwise arc elasticities range from about -0.1 to -0.5.'
    - id: keelerrolph1988
      title: The demand for episodes of treatment in the Health Insurance Experiment
      authors: Keeler EB, Rolph JE (Journal of Health Economics)
      year: 1988
      finding: 'Grouping claims into episodes: cost sharing lowered the number of episodes begun, with virtually no effect on cost per episode; arc elasticities of about -0.2, the source of RAND''s summary elasticity.'

    scratch design top-right work ["RAND, 1974–1982","6 sites","≈ 2,750 families","3 or 5 years"] font_size 38 hidden true
    scratch cap top-right work ["Yearly cap:","share of income,","≤ $1,000"] font_size 38 hidden true
