    label l_95 "95%: $700" above #b5482e attach_to d_95 cites ["newhouse1993"] font_size 36 hidden true
    dot d_25 at.data [25,826] cites ["newhouse1993"] radius 9 hidden true
    label l_25 "25%: $826" above attach_to d_25 cites ["newhouse1993"] font_size 34 hidden true
    path ci_50 #8f887c cites ["arondine2013"] points [[50,640],[50,890]] data true style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    dot d_50 #3d3833 at.data [50,764] cites ["newhouse1993"] radius 11 style.fill #f8f4ea hidden true
    label l_50 "50%: $764?" above attach_to d_50 cites ["newhouse1993"] font_size 34 hidden true
    text pct45 "+45%" #8a5fa8 at.data [84,790] cites ["manning1987"] font_size 48 hidden true
    scratch elas top-right cites ["keelerrolph1988"] work [{"tex":"\\varepsilon \\approx -0.2"},"price +10%","care −2%"] font_size 38 hidden true
    scratch stay top-right cites ["arondine2013"] work ["Joined and finished","free: 88%","95% plan: 63%"] font_size 38 hidden true

    card title "Four prices, one lottery"

RAND's experiment ran from 1974 to 1982, in s (@draw design_box design_line_1 design_line_2 design_line_3@)ix places from Dayton, Ohio, to Seattle, and enrolled about two thousand seven hundred and fifty families.
    draw axes

Each family was given a plan by lot, for three or five years: f (@highlight design_line_4 effect circle@)ree care, or paying twenty-five, fifty or ninety-five percent of every bill.
    draw design_line_4

    erase design

But nobody faced ruin: a family's payments for the year  (@draw cap_line_2@)stopped at a share of its income, and never  (@draw cap_line_3@)above a thousand dollars.
    draw cap_box cap_line_1

And families on the costlier plans were paid larger lump sums for joining, to make up for the bills they might face.
    highlight cap_box

Why randomize a price, and not coverage itself? Because in the early seventies Congress was debating national  (@highlight x_label@)health insurance, and the open question was how much patients should pay.
    highlight cap_line_3 effect circle

And a price comes in doses. Insured against uninsured gives one (@highlight y_label@) gap; four prices can trace a whole curve.
    erase cap

    quiz "Compared with families paying ninety-five percent of every bill, how much more did free-care families spend?" intro "First, a guess."
        right: About forty-five percent more: a clear response to price, though far from a flood.
        wrong: Picture paying almost the whole bill against paying nothing: people do cut back, but they still go when it matters.
        + About 45 percent more
        * About 5 percent more
        * About three times as much

Here are the results, per pers (@highlight y_label@)on per year, in 1991 dollars. Free care: about one thousand and twenty.
    draw d_free l_free

Paying ninety-five percent: about seven hundred. So free-care families spent some fo (@draw pct45@)rty-five percent more, a gap far too large to be chance.
    draw d_95 l_95

The twenty-five percent plan landed in between, at about eight hundred and twenty-five. The fifty percent pla (@draw ci_50 d_50 l_50@)n was small, and its number is rough: one re-analysis even puts it above the twenty-five.
    draw d_25 l_25

Join them, and you have something economists usually have to assume: a demand curve for medical (@highlight d_25 d_50@) care, traced out by a lottery.
    draw curve_0

And it bends. Most of the fall comes in the first st (@draw you@)ep, from free care to paying a quarter of the bill.
    highlight d_free d_25 effect circle

Move the point along the curve, from free care to the right, and watch where spending falls fastest.
    explore params ["p"] watch "Watch the point slide from free care toward the ninety-five percent plan, and see where spending falls fastest."

Steep near zero, gentler after: going from free to a little changes more than going from a little to a lot, though the small middle plans cannot pin the shape down.
    animate params.p.value 95 duration 4

    erase you

RAND summed it up as a price elasticity of about minus zero point two: make care ten percent dearer, and people use about two percent less.
    draw elas_box elas_line_1

That number is RAND's modelled summary, not the slope of this curve. Simple calculations from the plan averages give anything from about minus zero point one to  (@draw elas_line_2@)minus zero point five, and the cap made the real p (@draw elas_line_3@)rice lower than the coinsurance rate.
    highlight elas_line_1 effect circle

Where did the difference come from? Free care raised the share of people who used any care in a y (@highlight l_free@)ear from about seventy-six to ninety-three percent, and the share (@highlight l_95@) admitted to hospital from about eight to ten percent.
    erase elas pct45

The price acted on whether people started a cou (@highlight d_free@)rse of care at all. Once they had, what that co (@highlight d_95@)urse cost barely moved.
    highlight d_free d_95

One worry remains. The lottery chose the plans, but families chose whether to join, and whether to stay: of those offered free care,  (@draw stay_line_2@)eighty-eight percent joined and finished; of those offered the ninety-fiv (@draw stay_line_3@)e percent plan, sixty-three percent.
    draw stay_box stay_line_1

In 2013, Aron-Dine, Einav and Finkelstein redid the numbers assuming the worst about who stayed away. Whether people used care at all still responded to price; how big the dollar drop was, and whether hospital use moved at all, became uncertain.
    highlight stay_line_3 effect circle

## And health?
voice: male
sources:
    - id: brook1983
      title: Does Free Care Improve Adults' Health? Results from a Randomized Controlled Trial
      authors: Brook RH, Ware JE, Rogers WH, et al. (New England Journal of Medicine)
      year: 1983
      finding: 3,958 adults aged 14-61 randomized for 3 or 5 years. Free care improved vision for those with poor vision (about 0.2 Snellen lines) and lowered diastolic blood pressure about 3 mm Hg for low-income people with high blood pressure, reducing their calculated risk of early death; no significant effect on eight other measures for the average participant, with confidence intervals that rule out more than a minimal effect; subgroup intervals wider.
    - id: lohr1986
      title: 'Use of Medical Care in the RAND Health Insurance Experiment: Diagnosis- and Service-Specific Analyses in a Randomized Controlled Trial'
      authors: Lohr KN, Brook RH, Kamberg CJ, et al. (RAND R-3469)
      year: 1986
      finding: Cost sharing reduced the use of highly effective and of rarely effective medical care by about the same proportion.
    - id: rand2006
      title: 'The Health Insurance Experiment: A Classic RAND Study Speaks to the Current Health Care Reform Debate'
      authors: RAND Corporation (Research Brief RB-9174)
      year: 2006
      finding: 'Summary: for the poorest and sickest participants, free care was associated with about a 10 percent lower predicted risk of dying, through better blood pressure control.'
    - id: arondine2013
      title: The RAND Health Insurance Experiment, Three Decades Later
      authors: Aron-Dine A, Einav L, Finkelstein A (Journal of Economic Perspectives)
      year: 2013
      finding: 'Free care vs 95% coinsurance: share with any medical spending 93% vs 76%; share with any inpatient use 10.3% vs 7.9%.'

