    figure fig aspect 3:2 members ["null_line","null_lab","worse","better","worse_lab","better_lab","h_avg","r1_lab","r1_ci","r1_dot","r1_val","r2_lab","r2_ci","r2_dot","r3_lab","r3_ci","r3_dot","r4_lab","r4_ci","r4_dot","h_sub","r5_lab","r5_ci","r5_dot","r5_val","r6_lab","r6_ci","r6_dot","r6_val","r7_lab","r7_ci","r7_dot","r7_val"] hidden true

    card title "And health?"

On free care, the share of people using any care in a year rose from about seventy-six to ninety-three percent, and the share admitted to hospital from about eigh (@highlight null_line@)t to ten percent. Did all that care buy better health?
    draw null_line null_lab

RAND measured health as carefully as it measured bills: physical exams, bloo (@highlight worse_lab better_lab@)d tests and long questionnaires, at entry and again at the end.
    draw worse worse_lab better better_lab

The answer came in 1983, when Robert Brook's team reported on almost four thousa (@draw h_avg@)nd people aged fourteen to sixty-one. The drawing shows direction, not (@highlight better better_lab@) scale: to the right means healthier on free care.
    highlight null_lab

General health, physical functi (@draw r2_lab r2_ci r2_dot@)oning, mental health: fo (@draw r3_lab r3_ci r3_dot@)r the average participant, free care made n (@draw r1_val@)o detectable difference.
    draw r1_lab r1_ci r1_dot

Nor did a calculated risk of dying.
    draw r4_lab r4_ci r4_dot

And the ranges were tight. For the average participant, the authors could rule out anything more than a minimal effect, in either direction.
    highlight r1_ci r2_ci r3_ci r4_ci

    erase null_lab worse worse_lab

But two groups did gain. Low-income people with high blood pressure ended up with diastolic pressure about three millimetres of mercury lower on free care.
    draw h_sub r5_lab r5_ci r5_dot r5_val

And people with poor eyesight saw a little better, by about a fifth of a line on the eye chart.
    draw r6_lab r6_ci r6_dot r6_val

For them, RAND's own summary projects about a tenth lower risk of early death. That is a projection from blood pressure, not a count of deaths.
    draw r7_lab r7_ci r7_dot r7_val

    quiz "When a price made people use less care, which care did they give up?"
        intro: A question RAND asked next.
        right: Both, about equally: cost sharing cut highly effective care about as much as care that rarely helps.
        wrong: Who decides whether to go, and do they know yet what is wrong?
        * Mostly care that rarely helps
        * Mostly care that works
        + Both, in about equal measure

So a price trims care bluntly: patients decide whether to go before anyone knows what is wrong.
    highlight r5_lab r6_lab r7_lab

Yet most participants were working-age adults in fairly good health, with little that extra care could fix in three to five years.
    highlight h_avg

The gains showed up where there was something to fix, with wider ranges, because those groups were small.
    highlight r5_ci r6_ci r7_ci

So the average was flat, and the effects that did exist sat in a small corner of the sample, where the need was.
    highlight r5_val r6_val r7_val effect circle

## Reading the sentence
voice: male
sources:
    - id: brook1983
      title: Does Free Care Improve Adults' Health? Results from a Randomized Controlled Trial
      authors: Brook RH, Ware JE, Rogers WH, et al. (New England Journal of Medicine)
      year: 1983
      finding: '3,958 adults aged 14-61 without work-preventing disability, randomized for 3 or 5 years: no significant effect of free care on most health measures for the average participant (intervals rule out more than a minimal effect); improvements for low-income people with high blood pressure and for those with poor vision.'
    - id: lohr1986
      title: 'Use of Medical Care in the RAND Health Insurance Experiment: Diagnosis- and Service-Specific Analyses in a Randomized Controlled Trial'
      authors: Lohr KN, Brook RH, Kamberg CJ, et al. (RAND R-3469)
      year: 1986
      finding: Cost sharing reduced the use of highly effective and of rarely effective medical care by about the same proportion.
    - id: manning1987
      title: 'Health Insurance and the Demand for Medical Care: Evidence from a Randomized Experiment'
      authors: Manning WG, Newhouse JP, Duan N, Keeler EB, Leibowitz A, Marquis MS (American Economic Review)
      year: 1987
      finding: Spending per person on the free plan was about 45 percent higher than on the 95 percent coinsurance plan; price elasticity about -0.2.
    - id: baicker2013
      title: 'The Oregon Experiment: Effects of Medicaid on Clinical Outcomes'
      authors: Baicker K, Taubman SL, Allen HL, et al. (New England Journal of Medicine)
      year: 2013
      finding: 'Oregon''s 2008 lottery for Medicaid among uninsured low-income adults: about two years on, less depression and financial strain, no significant change in measured blood pressure or blood sugar.'
takeaways:
    - A lottery of prices draws a curve
    - 'Free care: about 45% more spending'
    - Flat average, gains for the sick poor

