    scratch sideB cites ["lohr1986"] work ["Critics:","good care cut too; too few years"] x 500 y 175 font_size 34 hidden true
    scratch settle work ["To settle it:","bigger, longer, sicker, uninsured"] x 500 y 175 font_size 34 hidden true

    card title "Reading the sentence"

So here is the sentence people carry away from RAND: more care did not improve average health.
    draw w1 w2 w3 w4

It is often quoted as proof that medical care barely matters. Each of its words is narrower than that.
    highlight w1 w2 w3 w4

More care means the extra visits that a ze (@draw t1@)ro price brought, among families who all had insurance of one kind or another.
    highlight w1

Did not improve means no change beyond a (@draw t2@) small margin, on these measures, over three to five years.
    highlight w2

Average hides who gained: the poor w (@draw t3@)ith high blood pressure, and people with poor eyesight.
    highlight w3

And health means the health of people aged fourteen to s (@draw t4@)ixty-one who were able to work: none of the elderly, and no one was left uninsured.
    highlight w4

Economists still read the result two ways. RAND's own team: for a typical adult, the e (@draw sideA_line_2@)xtra care bought little measurable health, so moderate co (@highlight t2@)st sharing saves money at small cost to health.
    draw sideA_box sideA_line_1

    erase sideA

Critics answer that even RAND's own data show the price cut e (@draw sideB_line_2@)ffective care as much as useless care, that the poo (@highlight t3@)r and sick did lose, and that a few years is too short for heart disease or cancer to show.
    draw sideB_box sideB_line_1

    erase sideB

What would tell them apart is a much larger trial of peop (@draw settle_line_2@)le at high risk, followed for longer, or a trial among the uninsured.
    draw settle_box settle_line_1

Oregon's 2008 lottery, which handed out Medicaid to uninsured low-income adults by drawing names, was a step that way.
    highlight t4

    erase settle

    quiz "A state adds a copay for doctor visits for low-income adults, many with high blood pressure. Which reading of RAND fits?" intro "One new case."
        right: Visits will fall, and these are the very people whose blood pressure free care lowered in RAND, so their control may slip.
        wrong: In RAND, whose health did respond to free care, and does this group look like them?
        * Health won't change: RAND found no effect
        + Visits will fall, and blood pressure control may slip
        * Visits won't change much

    erase w1 w2 w3 w4 t1 t2 t3 t4

So why randomize the price of car (@draw takeaway_1@)e? Because the price was the policy question, and a lottery of prices drew (@highlight takeaway_1@) a demand curve that no comparison of chosen plans could.

Free care raised spend (@draw takeaway_2@)ing by about forty-five percent, mostly by bringing (@highlight takeaway_2@) more people to start a course of care.

And the famous null holds for (@draw takeaway_3@) the average, fairly healthy adult at that margin, and breaks as (@highlight takeaway_3@) soon as it is stretched to the poor and the sick.

Read with every word attached, the sentence does not say that care doesn't matter. It says that a price is a blunt way to decide who gets it.
    highlight takeaway_3

## Where next
